@meta
id: 25-08
title: From one pattern to another
chapter: 25
type: lesson
skills: range-pattern
par: 23
minutes: 5
vscode: click the first marker, scroll, shift-click the last, Delete
open: container.ts

@brief
The generated registration block in `container.ts` is dead. A loader
walks the directory at boot now, and the block between the two markers —
markers included — is what has to go.

Both ends of a range are addresses, and either end can be a pattern. So
a block delimited by two lines you can search for is a single range:
left address finds the top, right address finds the bottom.

You do not need to know how many lines are between them, and the command
keeps working when the generator adds three more.

@keys
| `:/BEGIN/,/END/d` | from the next `BEGIN` line to the next `END` line after it |
| `:/a/,/b/y` | yank that block |
| `:/a/,/b/>` | indent it |

@hints
Two pattern addresses with a comma between them. The markers are in
capitals and each word appears once.
--
The right-hand pattern is searched for starting below the line the
left-hand one found, so the two cannot cross.
--
`:/BEGIN/,/END/d` Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    :/BEGIN/,/END/d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty keystrokes for a block of any length, and both marker lines go
with it because a range includes both of its ends.

The ordering rule is the thing to remember: the **left** address is
resolved from the cursor, and the **right** address is then resolved from
the line the left one landed on. That is why `/BEGIN/,/END/` cannot
accidentally select backwards, and why running it again finds the *next*
pair rather than the same one.

If a range does end up backwards — `:10,5d`, or two patterns that resolve
out of order — vim asks `Backwards range given, OK to swap (y/n)?`
instead of doing something surprising. Answer `n` and think again.

Keeping the markers is the `+1`/`-1` variant from the last lesson:

    :/BEGIN/+1,/END/-1d       empty the block, leave the markers
    :/BEGIN/+1,/END/-1s/x/y/g rewrite only what is inside it

@start container.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';
import { loadRegistrations } from './configuration/loader';

container.register(PrismaClient, { useValue: new PrismaClient() });

// BEGIN GENERATED — do not edit by hand
container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('RefundProvider', { useClass: RefundProvider });
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('BatchProvider', { useClass: BatchProvider });
// END GENERATED
loadRegistrations(container, './src/domain');

export { container };

@expected container.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';
import { loadRegistrations } from './configuration/loader';

container.register(PrismaClient, { useValue: new PrismaClient() });

loadRegistrations(container, './src/domain');

export { container };
